<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taksin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1734">1734</a>–<a href="/years/1782">1782</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as King Taksin, Taksin the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taksin was a Siamese general of Chinese descent who rallied resistance after the Burmese destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> in 1767 and was crowned king at Thonburi. In a series of campaigns he drove out the Burmese and reunified Siam, bringing rival regimes such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phitsanulok">Phitsanulok</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phimai">Phimai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhon-si-thammarat">Nakhon Si Thammarat</a> under his rule. Deposed in 1782 amid accusations of erratic behaviour, he was executed, and his general Chakri became King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taksin.jpg" alt="Taksin" width="320" height="476" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tmd · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Taksin_%26_Phraya_Pichai_Dabhak_Monument_in_Wat_Kungtapao_002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Siam briefly broke into rival regimes at Phitsanulok, Phimai, Sawangkhaburi and Nakhon Si Thammarat, until the general <b>Taksin</b>, who had escaped the siege, drove out the remaining Burmese garrison, founded a new capital at Thonburi and reunited the kingdom, whose successor, the Chakri dynasty's Bangkok, continues the Siamese…</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Konbaung wars reshaped mainland Southeast Asia: the destruction of Ayutthaya in 1767 cleared the way for the rise of Thonburi and Bangkok under <b>Taksin</b> and the Chakri dynasty, and the Burmese victories over the Qing are remembered as one of the most successful defences against Chinese invasion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Harsh Burmese rule provoked northern lords, led by Kawila of Lampang and Phraya Chaban, to side with <b>King Taksin</b> of Thonburi, whose forces under Chao Phraya Chakri helped take Chiang Mai in 1775.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Taksin</b>, a provincial governor of partly Chinese descent, rallied resistance, expelled the occupying garrisons and ruled from Thonburi on the west bank of the Chao Phraya River, but by 1782 his rule had become erratic and a revolt broke out in the capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>About 30,000 Siamese were taken to Burma; <b>Taksin</b> later moved the royal seat to Thonburi.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-7">7 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Taksin</b> of the Thonburi Kingdom sacked Ha Tien in 1771, and the Tay Son wars further damaged it.</blockquote><a href="/state/ha-tien-principality">Ha Tien Principality</a></li></ul></section>
